"Noise sensitivity of crossings for high temperature Ising model | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2505.11457)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; @misc{tassion2025noisesensitivitycrossingshigh, title={Noise sensitivity of crossings for high temperature Ising model}, author={Vincent Tassion and Hugo Vanneuville}, year={2025}, eprint={2505.11457}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.PR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11457}, }\nSubmitted 06 Aug 2025\n\n# Noise sensitivity of crossings for high temperature Ising model\n\nETH Z urichImageCNRS Universit Grenoble Alpes\n\nVT\n\nVincent Tassion\n\nHV\n\nHugo Vanneuville\n\n## Abstract\n\nConsider the event that there is a $+$+ crossing from left to right in a box for the Ising model on the triangular lattice. We show that this event is noise sensitive under Glauber dynamics $t \\mapsto \\sigma_t$t↦σ t​ in the subcritical regime \\beta&lt;\\beta_c. We rely on the non-spectral approach from our previous work [TV23]. An important aspect in this more general setup is the study of the pair $(\\sigma_0,\\sigma_t)$(σ 0​,σ t​) and in particular the establishment of properties such as finite-energy and spatial mixing.\n\nView Paper\n\n1\n\nCite\n\n15 views\n\nv2\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{tassion2025noisesensitivitycrossingshigh, title={Noise sensitivity of crossings for high temperature Ising model}, author={Vincent Tassion and Hugo Vanneuville}, year={2025}, eprint={2505.11457}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.PR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11457}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCritical phenomena for a triangular spin lattice I: Triplet interactions - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0378437176901035)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Critical phenomena in an Ising-like spin system on a triangular lattice are studied by means of the renormalization theory.The critical indices of the critical point due to triplet interactions are determined approximately, in fair agreement with the exponents, known from the exact solution due to Baxter and Wu.\n\n## Physica A: Statistical Mechanics and its Applications\n\nVolume 84, Issue 3, 1976, Pages 539-556\n\n# Critical phenomena for a triangular spin lattice I: Triplet interactions\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/0378-4371(76)90103-5Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nCritical phenomena in an Ising-like spin system on a triangular lattice are studied by means of the renormalization theory. The critical indices of the critical point due to triplet interactions are determined approximately, in fair agreement with the exponents, known from the exact solution due to Baxter and Wu. The cross-over from triplet critical behavior to ferromagnetic (nearest neighbor) critical behavior is calculated.\n\n\n## References (7)\n\n  * Th. Niemeijer et al.K. Subbarao\n\n### Phys. Rev.\n\n(1975)\n\n  * L. Onsager\n\n### Phys. Rev.\n\n(1944)\n\n  * R.J. Baxter et al.\n\n### Phys. Rev. Letters\n\n(1973)\n\n### Austr. J. Phys.\n\n(1974)\n\nR.J. Baxter et al.\n\n### J. Phys. A\n\n(1975)\n\nThere are more references available in the full text version of this article.\n\n## Cited by (16)\n\n  * ### Monte Carlo study of the pure and dilute Baxter-Wu model\n\n2005, Journal of Physics A Mathematical and General\n\n  * ### Monte Carlo renormalization-group study of the impure Baxter-Wu model\n\n1985, Physical Review B\n\n  * ### Monte Carlo renormalization-group study of the Baxter-Wu model\n\n1982, Physical Review B\n\n  * ### Critical behavior of the Baxter-Wu model with quenched impurities\n\n1981, Physical Review B\n\nView full text\n\nCopyright © 1976 Published by Elsevier B.V.\n\n\n### Citations\n\n  * Citation Indexes 16\n\n### Captures\n\n  * Mendeley Readers 1\n\nImage: PlumX Metrics LogoView details--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCritical phenomena for a triangular spin lattice I (https://ideas.repec.org/a/eee/phsmap/v84y1976i3p539-556.html)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Critical phenomena in an Ising-like spin system on a triangular lattice are studied by means of the renormalization theory.The critical indices of the critical point due to triplet interactions are determined approximately, in fair agreement with the exponents, known from the exact solution due to Baxter and Wu.\n\n# Critical phenomena for a triangular spin lattice I\n\n## Author\n\nListed:\n\n  * Den Nijs, M.P.M.\n  * Pruisken, A.M.M.\n  * Van Leeuwen, J.M.J.\n\nRegistered:\n\n## Abstract\n\nCritical phenomena in an Ising-like spin system on a triangular lattice are studied by means of the renormalization theory. The critical indices of the critical point due to triplet interactions are determined approximately, in fair agreement with the exponents, known from the exact solution due to Baxter and Wu. The cross-over from triplet critical behavior to ferromagnetic (nearest neighbor) critical behavior is calculated.\n\n## Suggested Citation\n\n* Den Nijs, M.P.M. & Pruisken, A.M.M. & Van Leeuwen, J.M.J., 1976. \"Critical phenomena for a triangular spin lattice I,\" Physica A: Statistical Mechanics and its Applications, Elsevier, vol. 84(3), pages 539-556.\nHandle: RePEc:eee:phsmap:v:84:y:1976:i:3:p:539-556\nDOI: 10.1016/0378-4371(76)90103-5\n\n\n## Download full text from publisher\n\n[Input][Input]File URL: http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0378437176901035\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlternating Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar Ising Model (https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.00985)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.4 years ago; Title: Alternating Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar Ising ModelAuthors: Hao Wu\nTitle: Alternating Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar Ising Model\nAuthors: Hao Wu\nDate: Tue May  3 16:59:51 2016\n\nWe derive the alternating arm exponents of critical Ising model. We obtain six different patterns of alternating boundary arm exponents which correspond to the boundary conditions $(\\ominus\\oplus)$, $(\\ominus\\text{free})$ and $(\\text{free}\\text{free})$, and the alternating interior arm exponents.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZero-temperature phase transitions of an antiferromagnetic Ising model of general spin on a triangular lattice | Phys. Rev. B (https://journals.aps.org/prb/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevB.55.14935)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; We map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface.\n\n# Zero-temperature phase transitions of an antiferromagnetic Ising model of general spin on a triangular lattice\n\nChen Zeng\n\nChristopher L. Henley\n\n  * ^{}Department of Physics, Syracuse University, Syracuse, New York 13210\n\n  * ^{}Department of Physics, Cornell University, Ithaca, New York 14853\n\nPDF\n\nPhys. Rev. B 55, 14935 – Published 1 June, 1997\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.55.14935\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface. Monte Carlo simulations for the ground-state ensemble utilizing this interfacial representation are performed to study both the dynamical and the static properties of the model. This method yields more accurate numerical results for the critical exponents. By varying the spin magnitude in the model, we find that the model exhibits three phases with a Kosterlitz-Thouless phase transition at <{\\mathrm{S}}_{\\mathrm{KT}}<2 and a locking phase transition at <{\\mathrm{S}}_{\\mathrm{L}}⩽3. The phase diagram at finite temperatures is also discussed.\n\nPhys. Rev. B 55, 14935– Published 1 June, 1997\n\nVol. 55, Iss. 22 — 1 June 1997\n\n[Button: Export Citation]\n\nReuse & Permissions\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.55.14935\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCriticality in the randomness-induced second-order phase transition of the triangular Ising antiferromagnet with nearest- and next-nearest-neighbor interactions - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physa.2009.08.022)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; The generalized (pure) Ising Hamiltonian is described by the following 𝐻𝑝=𝐽𝑛⁢𝑛⁢∑⟨𝑖,𝑗⟩𝑆𝑖⁢𝑆𝑗+𝐽𝑛⁢𝑛⁢𝑛⁢∑(𝑖,𝑗)𝑆𝑖⁢𝑆𝑗, and the Ising spin system is embedded on a 2d triangular lattice. ... The effects of the exchange interaction (J), crystal field (D), concentration (p) and temperature (T) on the magnetic properties of the spin-1 BC model in a triangular lattice, such as magnetization, susceptibility, phase diagram and hysteresis behaviors, are investigated in detail. ... By applying the finite-size scaling analysis, the critical exponents are estimated; the specific heat exponent vanishes, the correlation-length exponent 𝜈 is equal to one and the critical exponents’ ratio (𝛾/𝜈) retains its pure-Ising-model value, thus supporting the strong universality hypothesis.\nThe emerging, under random bonds, continuous transition shows a strongly saturating specific heat behavior, corresponding to a negative exponent 𝛼, and belongs to a new distinctive universality class with 𝜈 =1.135⁢(11), 𝛾/𝜈 =1.744⁢(9), and 𝛽/𝜈 =0.124⁢(8). Thus, our results for the critical exponents support an extensive but weak universality and the emerged continuous transition has the same magnetic critical exponent (but a different thermal critical exponent) as a wide variety of two-dimensional (2d) systems without and with quenched disorder.\n\n## Introduction\n\nUnderstanding the role played by impurities on the nature of phase transitions is of major importance, both from experimental and theoretical perspectives. It has been known that quenched bond randomness may or may not modify the critical exponents of second-order phase transitions, based on the Harris criterion [1], [2]. It was more recently established that quenched bond randomness always affects first-order phase transitions by conversion to second-order phase transitions, for infinitesimal randomness in 𝑑 =2 [3], [4] and after a threshold amount of randomness in 𝑑 >2 [4], as also inferred by general arguments [5].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZero-Temperature Phase Transitions of Antiferromagnetic Ising Model of General Spin on a Triangular Lattice (https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/9609007)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 30.1 years ago; We map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface.\nTitle: Zero-Temperature Phase Transitions of Antiferromagnetic Ising Model of General Spin on a Triangular Lattice\nAuthors: C. Zeng, C. L. Henley\nDate: Sat Aug 31 20:19:58 1996\n\nWe map the ground-state ensemble of antiferromagnetic Ising model of spin-S on a triangular lattice to an interface model whose entropic fluctuations are proposed to be described by an effective Gaussian free energy, which enables us to calculate the critical exponents of various operators in terms of the stiffness constant of the interface. Monte Carlo simulations for the ground-state ensemble utilizing this interfacial representation are performed to study both the dynamical and the static properties of the model. This method yields more accurate numerical results for the critical exponents. By varying the spin magnitude in the model, we find that the model exhibits three phases with a Kosterlitz-Thouless phase transition at 3/2<S_{KT}<2 and a locking phase transition at 5/2 < S_L \\leq 3. The phase diagram at finite temperatures is also discussed.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolychromatic Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar FK-Ising model (https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.06639)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.5 years ago; Authors: Hao Wu ... We obtain six different patterns of boundary arm exponents and three different patterns of interior arm exponents of the critical planar FK-Ising model on the square lattice.\nTitle: Polychromatic Arm Exponents for the Critical Planar FK-Ising model\nAuthors: Hao Wu\nDate: Fri Apr 22 12:59:21 2016\n\nSchramm Loewner Evolution (SLE) is a one-parameter family of random planar curves introduced by Oded Schramm in 1999 as the candidates for the scaling limits of the interfaces in the planar critical lattice models. This is the only possible process with conformal invariance and a certain \"domain Markov property\". In 2010, Chelkak and Smirnov proved the conformal invariance of the scaling limits of the critial planar FK-Ising model which gave the convergence of the interface to SLE$_{16/3}$. We derive the arm exponents of SLE$_{\\kappa}$ for $\\kappa\\in (4,8)$. Combining with the convergence of the interface, we derive the arm exponents of the critical FK-Ising model. We obtain six different patterns of boundary arm exponents and three different patterns of interior arm exponents of the critical planar FK-Ising model on the square lattice.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPhase transitions of two spin-1/2 Baxter–Wu layers coupled with Ising-type interactions (https://ctp.itp.ac.cn/EN/10.1088/1572-9494/abc7ac)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; Crawled: today; Phase transitions of two spin-1/2 Baxter–Wu layers coupled with Ising-type interactions ... Using a Monte Carlo simulation and the single histogram reweighting technique, we study the critical behaviors and phase transitions of the Baxter–Wu (BW) model on a two-layer triangular lattice with Ising-type interlayer couplings. ... where, the spin variables are located at the vertices of the lattice within layer A or B and take the values . ⟨ijk⟩ denotes the product of the three spins on the elementary triangle, and the sum is over all triads ⟨ijk⟩.\n\nPhase transitions of two spin-1/2 Baxter–Wu layers coupled with Ising-type interactions\n\nWei Liu,Zhengxin Yan,Yixian Wang\n\nCommunications in Theoretical Physics ›› 2021, Vol. 73 ›› Issue (1) : 15602.\n\nImage PDF(671 KB)\n\nCommunications in Theoretical Physics ›› 2021, Vol. 73 ›› Issue (1) : 15602. DOI: 10.1088/1572-9494/abc7ac  CSTR: 32041.14.CTP.1572-9494/abc7ac\n\nStatistical Physics, Soft Matter and Biophysics\n\nPhase transitions of two spin-1/2 Baxter–Wu layers coupled with Ising-type interactions\n\n  * Wei Liu^{weiliu@xust.edu.cn}, \n  * Zhengxin Yan, \n  * Yixian Wang\n\nAuthor information +\n\nCollege of Science, Xi’an University of Science and Technology, Xi’an 710054, China\n\nShow less\n\nHistory +\n\nReceived  | Revised  | Accepted  | Published\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n2020-06-05  | 2020-10-13  | 2020-11-04  | 2021-01-01\nIssue Date  |  \n--- | ---\n2021-01-26  |  \n\n### Abstract\n\nUsing a Monte Carlo simulation and the single histogram reweighting technique, we study the critical behaviors and phase transitions of the Baxter–Wu (BW) model on a two-layer triangular lattice with Ising-type interlayer couplings. Via the finite-size analysis, we obtain the transition temperatures and critical exponents at repulsive and attractive interlayer couplings. The data for the repulsive interlayer coupling suggest continuous transitions, and the critical behaviors are the same as those of the 2D BW model, belonging to the four-state Potts universality class. The reduced energy cumulants and the histograms reveal that attractive coupling leads to weak first-order phase transitions. The pseudocritical exponents with the existence of the interlayer couplings indicate that the first-order transition is very close to the critical point of the 2D standard BW model.\n\n### Key words\n\nBaxter–Wu model / Monte Carlo simulation / critical behavior / weak first order phase transition\n\nWei Liu, Zhengxin Yan, Yixian Wang. Phase transitions of two spin-1/2 Baxter–Wu layers coupled with Ising-type interactions[J]. Communications in Theoretical Physics, 2021, 73(1): 15602 https://doi.org/10.1088/1572-9494/abc7ac\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Noise sensitivity of crossings for high temperature Ising model (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/391857276_Noise_sensitivity_of_crossings_for_high_temperature_Ising_model)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Noise sensitivity of crossings for high temperature Ising model ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2505.11457\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Noise sensitivity of crossings for high temperature Ising model\n\n  * May 2025\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2505.11457\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nVincent Tassion\n\nVincent Tassion\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nHugo Vanneuville\n\nHugo Vanneuville\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer revie